    "turn_text": "you know out of the shadows and uh... certainly uh... folks who came here as children we're not uh... we're not documented at the time like i was you know i was born in rhodile my parents came as you know uh... immigrants through uh... you know the the normal channels but they're complicated stories all around right and so for me The thing that's really important is that we actually have a comprehensive set of solutions that we are focused and fixated on what we can do to deal with, you know, the deal with folks who should not be here because they've committed some crime. They've, you know, sort of not passed the test, but that we also think about the whole of our country and our economy, right? You know, a lot. of folks who come to this country, help power our economy in so many ways. And so when I look at what the president has done, arbitrarily profiling folks and picking them up, and many times as we've seen in the headlines, people with legal status who are entitled to be here, even having US citizens be scooped up. That's a problem when you have a quota-based arbitrary profiling basis to running your immigration system. So I would be more satisfied with an approach that looked at all of the aspects and that was thoughtful and reflective because making mistakes can really damage lives irreparably. And we have to think about our values first and foremost as Americans. E pluribus, you know, out of many one. We are, that is the model of our country. We are a nation of immigrants. People came from all over the place. Whether you came one generation ago, like my parents, or people who came far longer, In Rhode Island, we've got a rich tapestry from Irish, to Italian, Portuguese, to Cape Verde, to my families and people from West Africa. So we just have to have a reasonable and thoughtful approach here, because that's part of our",
